artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a quiescent solar prominence whose fine structure, as seen in the 304 Å He II channel, contains oblique thread-like features aligned with magnetic field lines that lie along large coronal arches. These threads display rapid plasma motions of cool (chromospheric-temperature, ~10^4 K) material, with apparent opposite-direction (counter-streaming) flows along neighboring threads, embedded in a hotter EUV-emitting (~10^5–10^6 K) environment. Three candidate physical mechanisms have been proposed to account for these observed motions:

(i) rapid radiative cooling and condensation of EUV-emitting coronal plasma into cool material that then streams along the inclined field lines;
(ii) longitudinal oscillations of cool plasma trapped in magnetic dips, in which neighboring dipped flux tubes oscillate with different phases so that material travels toward one footpoint on some threads and toward the opposite footpoint on others, producing apparent counter-streaming;
(iii) large-scale pressure-driven siphon flows between the two footpoints of the supporting magnetic structure when these footpoints are rooted in regions of unequal magnetic field strength (and hence unequal chromospheric pressure), producing net mass transport from one end of the prominence to the other.

Determine which of mechanisms (i), (ii), (iii) — singly or in combination — is responsible for the fast cool-plasma flows and the observed counter-streaming along the oblique threads of large arch-like prominences. In particular, identify quantitative observational and theoretical criteria (characteristic velocities, timescales, thermal evolution, footpoint magnetic asymmetry, oscillation periods) that discriminate among the three scenarios, and decide whether the dominant mechanism for large-scale prominence structures is siphon flow rather than oscillation-induced counter-streaming or condensation from the EUV phase.
